s value. Beyond this country lies another called Malal, the king of which was sincerely attached to Islam, while the common people of his kingdom remained polytheists. Since then their rulers have been given the title of al-musulmani .", "out": "$passage_note"}, {"type": "refine", "target": "$passage_note", "instruction": "Extract all evidence from the text that suggests trade connections with Asia, focusing on materials, goods, or cultural practices associated with Asian regions. List each piece of evidence clearly.", "out": "$evidence_extracted"}, {"type": "think", "value": "Let me analyze each answer choice against the evidence I extracted:\n\nEvidence from text:\n1. 'All other people wear robes of cotton, silk, or brocade, according to their means'\n2. 'The king adorns himself like a woman, wearing necklaces round his neck and bracelets on his forearms'\n3. 'On every donkey-load of salt when it is brought into the country their king levies one golden dinar'\n4. 'From a load of copper the king's due is five mithqals, and from a load of other goods ten mithqals'\n\nChoice A: 'They kept horses in their court, which would have come from the Mongols.' - The text mentions horses but says nothing about their origin or that they came from Mongols. This is not supported by the text.\n\nChoice B: 'The Ghanian king had adopted the Chinese tradition of the kow-tow.' - The text describes a greeting ritual where people fall on their knees and sprinkle dust, but this is described as the general way of greeting, not specifically the Chinese kow-tow. Also, the Muslims greet differently, so it's not clear if this is a Chinese tradition. Not clearly supported.\n\nChoice C: 'The king adorned himself with gold and jewels, probably from India.' - The text mentions the king wears necklaces and bracelets, but doesn't specify the origin of the gold/jewels. While it's possible they came from India, this is an assumption not directly stated in the text.\n\nChoice D: 'The people in his court wore silk robes.' - This directly matches evidence #1 from the text. The text explicitly states people wear robes of silk.\n\nSo Choice D is most directly supported by the text."}], "error_count": 0}
