t the ruler was selecting individuals for positions based on their demonstrated abilities and achievements rather than just birth or social status. This aligns with the concept of meritocracy, where advancement is based on individual ability and performance. This is a key aspect of bureaucratic organization. The other options (B, C, D) are not as clearly supported by the text. The text doesn't emphasize long-distance contact, clan structure, or a lack of martial emphasis. The focus is clearly on the ruler's recognition of military merit and subsequent appointment.", "out": "$thought"}], "error_count": 0}
{"outcome": true, "goal": "  subject: high_school_world_history\n  question: This question refers to the following information.\nI walk alongside the column, ask what's going on.\nA soldier says simply: \"They call up more every day.\n\"Some of us were sent north to the Yellow River at age fifteen,\nAnd now at forty we're heading off to the garrisons in the west.\nOn our first tour, the village headman had to tie our bandannas for us.\nWhen we came back, our hair was white, but still there's more unrest.\nThe frontier garrisons run with blood, enough to fill an ocean,\nBut the Martial Emperor's territorial ambitions have yet to crest.\nIn the hundred districts east of the mountains, throughout the land of Han,\nThere must be ten thousand villages that brambles now infest.\nEven if wives are strong enough to handle a hoe and plow,\nThe crops grow every which way, the fields are all a mess.\nIt's hardest for the Shanxi men, with their reputations as fighters:\nThey're rounded up like dogs or chickens, every male impressed.\n\"But sir, though it's good of you to ask,\nComplaining isn't part of the soldier's task.\nWe can only shake our heads. Take this winter:\nThe Shanxi troops were never sent home.\nThe District Officers are demanding the land tax,\nBut where will it come from? You can't get blood from a stone!\nI honestly think it's bad luck to bear a son now,\nIt's better to have a daughter: at least she can marry\nAnd live with the neighbors next door.\nBut a son will end up lying on some distant prairie.\"\nDu Fu, \"Ballad of the Army Carts,\" ca. 750 C.E.\nBased on the poem and your knowledge of history, Tang relations with western nomadic peoples and frontier peoples relied on which of the following?\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knowledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: Adroit diplomacy and establishment of client relationships with bordering nomads\n    B: The ability of the Tang emperor to project military power on the frontier in order to impose his will\n    C: The permanent settlement of Han Chinese in frontier zones\n    D: The payment of tribute to nomadic rulers\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "refine", "target": "Note_1234567890abcdef", "instruction": "Extract key elements from the poem that describe Tang Dynasty relations with western nomadic peoples and frontier peoples, focusing on military strategy, governance, and power projection.", "out": "$extracted_elements"}, {"type": "think", "value": "Let me analyze the key phrases from the poem that relate to Tang strategy with frontier peoples: 1. 'They call up more every day' - suggests constant mobilization, not just diplomacy. 2. 'frontier garrisons run with blood' - emphasizes military conflict and force. 3. 'Martial Emperor's territorial ambitions have yet to crest' - shows focus on expansion through military might. 4. 'Shanxi men...rounded up like dogs or chickens' - forced conscription, showing imposition of will. 5. 'Complaining isn't part of the soldier's task' - submission to authority, not negotiation. 6. 'The Shanxi troops were never sent home' - continuous military deployment, not tribute or settlement. 7. 'District Officers are demanding the land tax' - control and resource extraction. This points strongly towards B: military power projection. Choice A (diplomacy) is not mentioned. Choice C (permanent settlement) is not mentioned. Choice D (tribute) is not mentioned. The poem emphasizes military force, conscription, and control, not diplomacy or tribute.", "out": "$analysis_thought"}], "error_count": 0}
{"outcome": true, "goal": "  subject: high_school_world_history\n  question: This question refers to the following information.\nAt the peak of their power, the domains of the Mongol khans, or rulers, made up a vast realm in which once-hostile peoples lived together in peace and virtually all religions were tolerated. . . . The law code first promulgated by Chinggis Khan ordered human interaction. The result was an important new stage in international contact. From eastern Europe to southern China, merchants and travelers could move across the well-policed Mongol domains without fear for their lives or property. The great swath of Mongol territory that covered or connected most of Europe, Asia, and the Middle East served as a bridge between the civilizations of the Eastern Hemisphere. The caravans and embassies that crossed the Mongol lands transmitted new food, inventions, and ideas from one civilized pool to others and from civilized pools to the nomadic peoples who served as intermediaries. Secure trade routes made for prosperous merchants and wealthy, cosmopolitan cities. They also facilitated the spread of foods [and] inventions . . . a major force for economic and social development and the enhancement of civilized life.\n—Robert Guisepi, 1992\nThe Mongol empire used which of the following to integrate its vast, geographically diverse area?\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knowledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: Emphasis on trade networks\n    B: Expansion of bureaucracy to reinforce dominance\n    C: The use of state-sponsored religion to legitimize the government\n    D: Expansion of an interregional canal system\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "refine", "target": "Note_1234567890abcdef", "instruction": "Extract the specific methods or strategies the Mongol empire used to integrate its vast, geographically diverse area", "out": "$integration_methods"}, {"type": "create-note", "value": "At the peak of their power, the domains of the Mongol khans, or rulers, made up a vast realm in which once-hostile peoples lived together in peace and virtually all religions were tolerated. . . . The law code first promulgated by Chinggis Khan ordered human interaction. The result was an important new stage in international contact. From eastern Europe to southern China, merchants and travelers could move across the well-policed Mongol domains without fear for their lives or property. The great swath of Mongol territory that covered or connected most of Europe, Asia, and the Middle East served as a bridge between the civilizations of the Eastern Hemisphere. The caravans and embassies that crossed the Mongol lands transmitted new food, inventions, and ideas from one civilized pool to others and from civilized pools to the nomadic peoples who served as intermediaries. Secure trade routes made for prosperous merchants and wealthy, cosmopolitan cities. They also facilitated the spread of foods [and] inventions . . . a major force for economic and social development and the enhancement of civilized life.", "out": "$mongol_passage"}, {"type": "refine", "target": "$mongol_passage", "instruction": "Extract the specific methods or strategies the Mongol empire used to integrate its vast, geographically diverse area", "out": "$integration_methods"}, {"type": "refine", "target": "$mongol_passage", "instruction": "Extract specific information about trade, movement of people, and commerce within the Mongol Empire that contributed to its integration", "out": "$trade_movement_info"}], "error_count": 0}
{"outcome": true, "goal": "  subject: college_mathematics\n  question: Let k be the number of real solutions of the equation e^x + x - 2 = 0 in the interval [0, 1], and let n be the number of real solutions that are not in [0, 1]. Which of the following is true?\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knowledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: k = 0 and n = 1\n    B: k = 1 and n = 0\n    C: k = n = 1\n    D: k > 1\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "create-note", "value": "Let f(x) = e^x + x - 2. We need to find the number of real solutions to f(x) = 0.", "out": "$function_note"}, {"type": "think", "value": "Let f(x) = e^x + x - 2. To analyze this function:\n1. Domain: f(x) is defined for all real x.\n2. Behavior at boundaries of [0,1]:\n   - f(0) = e^0 + 0 - 2 = 1 + 0 - 2 = -1\n   - f(1) = e^1 + 1 - 2 = e + 1 - 2 = e - 1 ≈ 2.718 - 1 = 1.718\n   Since f(0) < 0 and f(1) > 0, by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there is at least one root in (0,1).\n3. Monotonicity (derivative):\n   - f'(x) = d/dx(e^x + x - 2) = e^x + 1\n   - For all real x, e^x > 0, so e^x + 1 > 1 > 0\n   - Therefore, f'(x) > 0 for all x, meaning f(x) is strictly increasing on R.\n4. Consequences:\n   - Since f(x) is strictly increasing, it can have at most one zero.\n   - We already established it has at least one zero in (0,1).\n   - Therefore, f(x) has exactly one zero, which lies in (0,1).\n5. Conclusion:\n   - k = 1 (one solution in [0,1])\n   - n = 0 (no solutions outside [0,1])"}], "error_count": 0}
{"outcome": false, "goal": "  subject: college_mathematics\n  question: Up to isomorphism, how many additive abelian groups G of order 16 have the property that x + x + x + x = 0 for each x in G ?\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knowledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: 0\n    B: 1\n    C: 2\n    D: 3\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "State the Fundamental Theorem of Finite Abelian Groups and list all possible structures (up to isomorphism) of additive abelian groups of order 16.", "out": "$group_theory_recall"}, {"type": "think", "value": "The condition x + x + x + x = 0 for all x in G means that 4x = 0 for all x. This is equivalent to saying that the group has exponent dividing 4. The exponent of a group is the smallest positive integer n such that x^n = e for all x in G. For a direct product of cyclic groups, the exponent is the least common multiple (LCM) of the orders of the cyclic groups. So I need to check which of the groups Z_16, Z_8 × Z_2, Z_4 × Z_4, Z_4 × Z_2 × Z_2, Z_2 × Z_2 × Z_2 × Z_2 have exponent dividing 4."}, {"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "For each of the following abelian groups of order 16, calculate the exponent (the smallest positive integer n such that x^n = e for all x in the group) and determine if it divides 4: Z_16, Z_8 × Z_2, Z_4 × Z_4, Z_4 × Z_2 × Z_2, Z_2 × Z_2 × Z_2 × Z_2.", "out": "$exponent_analysis"}, {"type": "think", "value": "From the analysis: Z_16 (exponent 16) - No, Z_8 × Z_2 (exponent 8) - No, Z_4 × Z_4 (exponent 4) - Yes, Z_4 × Z_2 × Z_2 (exponent 4) - Yes, Z_2 × Z_2 × Z_2 × Z_2 (exponent 2) - No. So, exactly 2 groups satisfy the condition. The answer is C."}], "error_count": 0}
{"outcome": false, "goal": "  subject: college_mathematics\n  question: Suppose P is the set of polynomials with coefficients in Z_5 and degree less than or equal to 7. If the operator D sends p(x) in P to its derivative p′(x), what are the dimensions of the null space n and range r of D?\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knowledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: n = 1 and r = 6\n    B: n = 1 and r = 7\n    C: n = 2 and r = 5\n    D: n = 2 and r = 6\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "Explain the vector space P of polynomials with coefficients in Z_5 and degree less than or equal to 7, and the derivative operator D acting on it. Then apply the rank-nullity theorem to find the dimensions of the null space and range of D.", "out": "$explanation"}, {"type": "think", "value": "From the explanation: P has dimension 8. The derivative operator D maps P to P. The null space of D consists of polynomials p(x) such that D(p(x)) = 0. The derivative of a polynomial is 0 if and only if the polynomial is constant. So, the null space is the set of constant polynomials. A basis for the constant polynomials in P is {1}, so the null space has dimension 1. By the rank-nullity theorem: dim(P) = dim(null space) + dim(range). Therefore: 8 = 1 + dim(range), so dim(range) = 7. This means n = 1 and r = 7. This matches choice B."}], "error_count": 0}
