tiable everywhere. 2. f'(x) = e^x + 1 > 0 for all x, so f(x) is strictly increasing on all of R. 3. f(0) = -1 < 0 and f(1) ≈ 1.718 > 0. 4. By the Intermediate Value Theorem and the fact that f is strictly increasing, there is exactly one root in [0, 1]. So k = 1. 5. lim(x→-∞) f(x) = -∞ and lim(x→+∞) f(x) = +∞. 6. Since f is strictly increasing and goes from -∞ to +∞, it crosses the x-axis exactly once. We already found this root in [0, 1]. Therefore, there are no other roots outside [0, 1]. So n = 0. 7. Therefore, k = 1 and n = 0. This matches option B."}], "error_count": 0}
