 choice A says the block will not slide off when μ_s > kA/[(M+m)g], which is the opposite of what I derived. Let me re-read the question and choices more carefully. \n\nThe question is asking which of the following is TRUE. It says 'but will not slide off as long as the coefficient of static friction is greater than...'. This means the condition is μ_s > kA/[(M+m)g] for the case when it will NOT slide off. That's wrong - if μ_s > kA/[(M+m)g], then it will NOT slide off. If μ_s < kA/[(M+m)g], then it WILL slide off. So the condition should be μ_s ≥ kA/[(M+m)g] for it NOT to slide off. \n\nLooking at choice A again: 'but will not slide off as long as the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is greater than kA/[(M+m)g]' - This is saying that if μ_s > kA/[(M+m)g], then it will NOT slide off. This is correct logic. \n\nLet me recheck: \n- Maximum acceleration occurs at maximum displacement \n- For no sliding: friction force must be sufficient to provide the acceleration\n- f_s,max = μ_s mg ≥ ma_max = m(k/(M+m))A\n- Therefore: μ_s g ≥ (k/(M+m))A\n- Therefore: μ_s ≥ kA/(g(M+m))\n\nSo the condition for NO sliding is μ_s ≥ kA/(g(M+m)) or equivalently, the condition for SLIDING is μ_s < kA/(g(M+m)).\n\nChoice A says: will not slide off as long as μ_s > kA/[(M+m)g]. This is correct - if μ_s > kA/[(M+m)g], then it will not slide off. \n\nChoice B says: will not slide off as long as μ_s > kA/[(M+m)g], but sliding occurs at equilibrium - this is wrong.\n\nChoice C says: will not slide off as long as μ_s > (M+m)g/(kA) - this is wrong.\n\nChoice D says: will not slide off as long as μ_s > (M+m)g/(kA), but sliding occurs at equilibrium - this is wrong.\n\nTherefore, choice A is correct."}], "error_count": 0}
