ble:\n| K | L | ~K | ~K ≡ L | K · L | K · ~L | (K · L) ∨ (K · ~L) |\n|---|---|----|--------|-------|--------|-------------------|\n| T | T | F  | T      | T     | F      | T                 |\n| T | F | F  | F      | F     | T      | T                 |\n| F | T | T  | T      | F     | F      | F                 |\n| F | F | T  | F      | F     | F      | F                 |\n\nThe premise is ~K ≡ L (column 4) and the conclusion is (K · L) ∨ (K · ~L) (column 7).\n\nFor the argument to be valid, the conclusion must be true whenever the premise is true.\n\nRow 1: Premise = T, Conclusion = T -> Valid\nRow 2: Premise = F, Conclusion = T -> Doesn't affect validity\nRow 3: Premise = T, Conclusion = F -> Invalid!\nRow 4: Premise = F, Conclusion = F -> Doesn't affect validity\n\nThe argument is invalid because in Row 3, the premise is true but the conclusion is false. The counterexample is K=F, L=T."}], "error_count": 0}
