s (submandibular and sublingual) and lacrimal gland. When a lesion compresses the facial nerve at the stylomastoid foramen, it affects the entire nerve after it exits the skull. This means ALL of these functions would be lost. So, the patient would have: 1) Paralysis of facial muscles (ipsilateral), 2) Loss of taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue, 3) Decreased salivation (from submandibular/sublingual glands), 4) Decreased lacrimation (from lacrimal gland). Looking at the answer choices: A lacks sensory/autonomic functions. B includes motor and taste but misses autonomic functions. C includes motor, taste, and lacrimation but misses salivation. D includes motor, taste, lacrimation, and decreased salivation. This seems to be the most comprehensive answer. However, I should double-check if lacrimation is indeed controlled by the facial nerve or if it's primarily the trigeminal nerve. Let me reconsider the autonomic functions of the facial nerve. The facial nerve does control the lacrimal gland through the greater petrosal nerve, which is a branch of the facial nerve. So, lacrimation is indeed affected. The main autonomic functions of the facial nerve are: 1) Parasympathetic innervation to the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands (via chorda tympani and lingual nerve), and 2) Parasympathetic innervation to the lacrimal gland (via greater petrosal nerve). So, the compression at stylomastoid foramen would affect all these functions. The answer should be D."}], "error_count": 0}
