bserved score + SEM). Looking at the answer choices, choice B talks about individuals with a given 'cure score' (should be 'true score') having observed scores within 15 points of the true score. This aligns with the probabilistic interpretation. Choice A refers to the population mean, not individual true scores. Choice C misinterprets SEM as a percentage of scores being wrong. Choice D confuses SEM with confidence interval width (which would be 2*SEM = 30 points for 68% confidence). So B seems correct."}, {"type": "think"}], "error_count": 1}
