{"outcome": true, "goal": "  subject: virology\n  question: AIDS activism in the U.S. resulted in:\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knowledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: Food and Drug Administration reforms\n    B: Easier access to investigational drugs\n    C: Changes in the way medicine was practiced in the U.S.\n    D: All of the above\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "What were the key impacts of AIDS activism in the U.S. during the 1980s and 1990s on FDA reforms, access to investigational drugs, and medical practice?", "out": "$impact_overview"}, {"type": "refine", "target": "$impact_overview", "instruction": "Extract specific information about the impacts on FDA reforms, access to investigational drugs, and changes in medical practice. Present the information in a clear, structured format highlighting each area.", "out": "$refined_impacts"}, {"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "Explain how AIDS activism in the U.S. during the 1980s and 1990s led to specific changes in: 1) Food and Drug Administration (FDA) processes and regulations, 2) Access to investigational drugs for patients, and 3) The broader practice of medicine in the U.S.", "out": "$comprehensive_impacts"}, {"type": "refine", "target": "$comprehensive_impacts", "instruction": "Extract and elaborate specifically on the impacts of AIDS activism on: 1) Access to investigational drugs for patients, and 2) Changes in the broader practice of medicine in the U.S. Provide clear bullet points for each impact.", "out": "$detailed_impacts"}, {"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "How did AIDS activism in the U.S. during the 1980s and 1990s change the practice of medicine in the U.S.? Focus on specific shifts in medical culture, patient-doctor relationships, and clinical approaches.", "out": "$medical_practice_impact"}, {"type": "create-note", "value": {"FDA_Reforms": "AIDS activism pressured the FDA to accelerate drug approval timelines, implement expedited review processes, create expanded access programs, and establish the HIV/AIDS Therapeutic Committee. It also led to the Office of AIDS Research within NIH and a shift towards recognizing patient input in regulatory science.", "Investigational_Drugs": "Activists pushed for compassionate use programs and expanded access to experimental treatments outside of clinical trials, making it easier for patients with terminal illnesses to access investigational drugs when standard treatment options were unavailable.", "Medical_Practice": "The movement demanded greater urgency and accountability from the medical establishment, forcing physicians to confront prejudices and the slow pace of research. This led to faster drug approval processes, more aggressive clinical trial designs, and shifts in medical culture and patient-doctor relationships."}, "out": "$synthesis_note"}], "error_count": 0}
