{"outcome": true, "goal": "  subject: high_school_physics\n  question: A student is experimenting with a simple spring-block oscillator of spring constant k and amplitude A. The block attached to the spring has a mass of m. If the student places a small block of mass m on top of the original block, which of the following is true?\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knowledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: The small block is most likely to slide off when the original block is at maximum displacement from the equilibrium position, but will not slide off as long as the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is greater than kA/[(M+m)g].\n    B: The small block is most likely to slide off when the original block is at the equilibrium position, but will not slide off as long as the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is greater than kA/[(M+m)g].\n    C: The small block is most likely to slide off when the original block is at maximum displacement from the equilibrium position, but will not slide off as long as the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is greater than (M+m)g/(kA).\n    D: The small block is most likely to slide off when the original block is at the equilibrium position, but will not slide off as long as the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is greater than (M+m)g/(kA).\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "Explain the physics principles and equations needed to solve a spring-block oscillator problem with a top block that might slide off. Focus on: 1) Spring force and simple harmonic motion equations, 2) Maximum acceleration in oscillation, 3) Friction forces (static and kinetic), 4) Condition for slipping (comparing inertial force to maximum static friction). Use clea