erate the top block at the maximum acceleration of the system.\n\nThe maximum acceleration of the system (both blocks together) is a_max = k*A/(M+m)\n\nThe maximum friction force available for the top block is f_s_max = μ_s * m * g\n\nFor the top block to not slide: f_s_max ≥ m * a_max\nμ_s * m * g ≥ m * k * A / (M + m)\nμ_s * g ≥ k * A / (M + m)\nμ_s ≥ k * A / [g * (M + m)]\n\nThis gives us μ_s ≥ kA/[(M+m)g], which matches choice A.\n\nBut wait, when does sliding occur? It occurs when the required friction exceeds the maximum available friction, i.e., when μ_s < kA/[(M+m)g].\n\nAlso, when does the maximum acceleration occur? It occurs when the displacement is maximum (at the extremes of oscillation), not at equilibrium.\n\nSo the answer should be A. But let me double-check the math and the reasoning."}], "error_count": 0}
