{"outcome": true, "goal": "  subject: high_school_world_history\n  question: This question refers to the following information.\nWoman, wake up; the bell of reason is being heard throughout the whole universe; discover your rights. Enslaved man has multiplied his strength, [but] having become free, he has become unjust to his companion. Oh, women, women! When will you cease to be blind? What advantage have you received from the Revolution? A more pronounced scorn, a more marked disdain. If our leaders persist, courageously oppose the force of reason to their empty pretentions of superiority. Regardless of what barriers confront you, it is in your power to free yourselves!\nOlympe de Gouges, \"Declaration of the Rights of Woman and the Female Citizen,\" 1791\nThe independence? Nothing of what I hoped for was achieved. I had expected that my children would be able to have an education, but they did not get it. We were poor peasants then, we are poor peasants now. Nothing has changed. Everything is the same. The only thing is that we are free, the war is over, we work without fear—but apart from that, nothing has changed.\nHalima Ghomri, interviewed in the 1970s, after Algeria's war of independence\nWhich of the following observations about revolutions and gender is best supported by the first passage?\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knowledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: Revolutionary progressivism does not always concern itself with gender equality.\n    B: Women typically assume leadership roles during times of revolution.\n    C: Women rarely support revolutions and therefore receive little benefit from them.\n    D: Revolutionary movements are generally uninterested in women's rights.\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "refine", "target": "Note_123", "instruction": "Identify the main argument and key claims about women's rights and the revolution in this text.", "out": "$analysis_result"}, {"type": "create-note", "value": "Woman, wake up; the bell of reason is being heard throughout the whole universe; discover your rights. Enslaved man has multiplied his strength, [but] having become free, he has become unjust to his companion. Oh, women, women! When will you cease to be blind? What advantage have you received from the Revolution? A more pronounced scorn, a more marked disdain. If our leaders persist, courageously oppose the force of reason to their empty pretentions of superiority. Regardless of what barriers confront you, it is in your power to free yourselves!", "out": "$passage_note"}, {"type": "refine", "target": "$passage_note", "instruction": "Identify the main argument and key claims about women's rights and the revolution in this text.", "out": "$analysis_result"}, {"type": "think", "value": "Let me analyze each choice against the passage's argument that women were marginalized and mistreated by the Revolution, receiving scorn and disdain rather than benefits. Choice A says revolutionary progressivism does not always concern itself with gender equality - this aligns with the passage showing the Revolution focused on men's freedom but not women's rights. Choice B says women assume leadership roles during revolution - the passage doesn't mention women in leadership, and the tone suggests they were excluded. Choice C says women rarely support revolutions because they get little benefit - the passage doesn't say women don't support revolutions, it shows they got little benefit after a revolution. Choice D says revolutionary movements are generally uninterested in women's rights - this is stronger than the passage, which shows one specific instance (the French Revolution) where women's rights were not prioritized, not that revolutionary movements generally ignore women's rights. So A seems most directly supported."}], "error_count": 0}
